{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>And Moses said, etc.: 'Please, Adonai.'</b> After the arguments were exhausted for him, he turned to beg HaShem from the attribute of mercy, and therefore he is precise in saying 'to HaShem' — the Name of mercy — which he did not say previously, for all his earlier statements were 'to Elohim.' And he began to supplicate before Him and said <b>'please'</b> (bi), an expression of supplication and entreaty." "<b>Or</b> it may intend in His saying <b>'please'</b> (bi) — meaning, the reason they will not believe is because of the deficiency dependent on me, when they see me stammering, and I do not even know how to speak at all. And His saying <b>'also since yesterday</b>' etc. — he intended to say to Him that he thought through the agency of HaShem's speaking with him two or three times, his defect would be remedied and he would speak fluently; and since he saw that He had spoken once, also twice, also three times, and he was still not a man of words, his heart cried out to HaShem — for his blemish is permanent, and from now on they will surely not believe him, for they will say: if HaShem had appeared to him, He would have removed his defect."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:24:27.949318490Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}